{"items":[{"tags":["urlencode","jsonplaceholder"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":55839550,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1792225663,"creation_date":1556146025,"body":"Basic question but couldn&#39;t find an answer to this.\n\nThere is fake API testing tool located here\nhttps://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos\n\nWhen I add a query like this\nhttps://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos?userId=4 \n\nI get a response\n\nWhen I change the query to this\nhttps://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos?userId&lt;4\nit returns null\n\nHow would I query in the url userId that is less than 4?\n","excerpt":"There is fake API testing tool located here\nhttps://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos\n\nWhen I add a query like this\nhttps://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos? &hellip; userId=4 \n\nI get a response\n\nWhen I change the query to this\nhttps://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos?userId&lt;4\nit returns null\n\nHow would I query in the url userId that is less than 4? &hellip; ","title":"How to query an API greater than less than"},{"tags":["javascript","python","web-scraping"],"question_score":296,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":66878732,"is_answered":false,"question_id":8049520,"item_type":"answer","score":7,"last_activity_date":1791994657,"creation_date":1617139858,"body":"Try accessing the API directly\nA common scenario you&#39;ll see in scraping is that the data is being requested asynchronously from an API endpoint by the webpage. A minimal example of this would be the following site:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe general procedure is to use your browser&#39;s developer tools&#39; network tab to search the requests made by the page for keywords/substrings of the data you want to scrape. Often, you&#39;ll see an unprotected API request endpoint with a JSON payload that you can access directly with  or  modules. That&#39;s the case with the above runnable snippet which you can use to practice. After clicking &quot;run snippet&quot;, here&#39;s how I found the endpoint in my network tab:\n\nThis example is contrived; the endpoint URL will likely be non-obvious from looking at the static markup because it could be dynamically assembled, minified and buried under dozens of other requests and endpoints. The network request will also show any relevant request payload details like access token you may need.\nAfter obtaining the endpoint URL and relevant details, build a request in Python using a standard HTTP library and request the data:\n\nWhen you can get away with it, this tends to be much easier, faster and more reliable than scraping the page with Selenium, Playwright-Python, Scrapy or whatever the popular scraping libraries are at the time you&#39;re reading this post.\nIn many cases, even if the endpoint is secured, copying a token or cookies from the browser request over to your Python request will give you access.\nIf you&#39;re unlucky and the data hasn&#39;t arrived via an API request that returns the data in a nice format, it could be part of the original browser&#39;s payload in a  tag, either as a JSON string or (more likely) a JS object. For example:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThere&#39;s no one-size-fits-all way to obtain this data. The basic technique is to use BeautifulSoup to access the  tag text, then apply a regex or a parse to extract the object structure, JSON string, or whatever format the data might be in. Here&#39;s a proof-of-concept on the sample structure shown above:\n\nCheck out these resources for parsing JS objects that aren&#39;t quite valid JSON:\n\nHow to convert raw javascript object to python dictionary?\nHow to Fix JSON Key Values without double-quotes?\n\nHere are some additional case studies/proofs-of-concept where scraping was bypassed using an API:\n\nMy Playwright python script doesn&#39;t find input form fields\nHow can I scrape yelp reviews and star ratings into CSV using Python beautifulsoup\nBeautiful Soup returns None on existing element\nExtract data from  BeautifulSoup Python\nFetch data from https://www.sofascore.com/?\nScraping Bandcamp fan collections via POST (uses a hybrid approach where an initial request was made to the website to extract a token from the markup using BeautifulSoup which was then used in a second request to a JSON endpoint)\nScraping nba.com page with puppeteer (uses JS, but the technique is adaptable to Python)\nPuppeteer &quot;Target Close&quot; crash at random points\nWeb scraper for pokemon prices using Selenium\nHow can I improve the speed of my scraping program?\n\nIf all else fails, try one of the many dynamic scraping libraries listed in this thread.\n","excerpt":"A minimal example of this would be the following site:\n\n\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nfetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/1&quot;)\n  .then(res =&gt; {\n    if (! &hellip; After obtaining the endpoint URL and relevant details, build a request in Python using a standard HTTP library and request the data:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; import requests\n&gt;&gt;&gt; res = requests.get(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How can I scrape a page with dynamic content (created by JavaScript) in Python?"},{"tags":["reactjs","filter","tableau-api","select-options"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73437689,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1788703400,"creation_date":1661110818,"body":"I can&#39;t find how to filter a table in React with the Select (dropdown). I found an example on Google but they used Node but their code doesn&#39;t work on my React app. I prefer using components not class functions. This is my code. And I want to filter the table exemple by select options?:\n\n","excerpt":":\nconst [data, getData] = useState([])\nconst URL = &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&#39;\nuseEffect(() =&gt; {\nfetchData()\n}, [])\n\nconst fetchData = () =&gt; {\nfetch(URL)\n    .then((res) =&gt;\n        res.json &hellip; ","title":"How can I filter a table with select options in React (component function)?"},{"tags":["go","proxy","reverse-proxy","go-gin"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":52374659,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1785599901,"creation_date":1537213351,"body":"I&#39;m trying to create a reverse proxy that takes all requests and redirect them into a target url, like google.com or any other API. Unfortunately, all targets returns errors like page Not Found, or Forbidden Errors\n\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\n\nI spent many days trying to set appropriate headers but nothing worked so far. I think the headers were not set correctly but I don&#39;t how to copy headers from client and add it to the proxy.\n\nI&#39;m using a proxy instead of a redirect because I have to do some work with the body request and response!\n\nThanks in advance!\n","excerpt":"NewEanAPI() EanAPI {\r\n\treturn &amp;eanAPI{}\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (api *eanAPI) ReverseProxy(ctx *gin.Context) {\r\n    // proxy\r\n    forwardToHost := &quot;http://httpbin.org/&quot;\r\n    // I tried with &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Golang reverse proxy returns Not found or Forbidden Errors"},{"tags":["angular","typescript","angular21"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79983810,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1784719066,"creation_date":1784713427,"body":"I&#39;m experiencing a strange issue with Angular 21. My  request succeeds, the component state is updated correctly, but the template still displays wrong.\nEnvironment:\n\nAngular: 21.2.18\nNode.js: 24.12.0\nnpm: 11.6.2\nOS: Windows 11\n\nComponent:\n\nTemplate:\n\n:\n\nThe API request succeeds. Console output:\n\nThe browser &quot;Network tab&quot; also shows a successful response. But the HTML page just shows the content for the first time, then I reload page and it&#39;s gone.\n\nHas anyone experienced a similar issue with Angular 21?\nCould this be related to the new Vite builder, HMR, Node.js 24 compatibility, or change detection?\n","excerpt":"get-api.css&#39;\n})\nexport class GetApi implements OnInit {\n\n  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);\n\n  todoList: Todo[] = [];\n\n  ngOnInit(): void {\n    this.http\n      .get&lt;Todo[]&gt;(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"HttpClient updates component state, but template doesn&#39;t refresh after API response"},{"tags":["angular","typescript","angular21"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79983817,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79983810,"item_type":"answer","score":6,"last_activity_date":1784715746,"creation_date":1784715746,"body":"Since you are using Angular 21 which is by default zoneless, we need to use  when you want to use the variable in the DOM or View (So that the updates are reflected in the view) another reason to use signals is when the state change needs to have reactivity of updating other states, like using , , etc.\nYou should also simplify the API calls, by using the resource API, for example we can simplify the code using .\nTS:\n\nHTML:\n\n","excerpt":"TS:\nexport class GetApi implements OnInit {\n  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);\n  public todoResource = httpResource(() =&gt; `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos`);\n}\n\nHTML:\n@if(todoResource.error &hellip; ","title":"HttpClient updates component state, but template doesn&#39;t refresh after API response"},{"tags":["angular","ionic2","angular2-testing"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":45252158,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1783746365,"creation_date":1500710306,"body":"Below is my Service class which is taking a url which has a json format data.\n\nservice.ts\n\n\n\nBelow is my page1.ts file which uses the service and gets the data from the service.\n\npage1.ts\n\n\n\nPlease ignore any typo.\n\nBelow is my spec file for this page, where I want to test this asynchronous http get method\n\npage1.spec.ts\n\n\n\nNote: I have to create a mock for this service, based on the real service that I have used.I am not sure whether this mock is correct.Please have a look at this mock.The data provided in the  url in this mock is in another folder which is named as data.mock.ts.\n\nservice.mock.ts\n\n\n\ndata.mock.ts\n\n\n","excerpt":"Service {\n\n    public http: string;\n    constructor(private http: Http) { }\n\n    getGoogle():Observable&lt;any&gt; {\n        console.log(&quot;Inside service&quot;);\n\n\n      return this.http.get(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to mock a service that contains http request and returns an observable?"},{"tags":["flutter","dart","asynchronous"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":75119546,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1780660676,"creation_date":1673714300,"body":"\nBased on documentation, i should just import async package and use unawaited().\nHowever, i noticed that the unawaited() only take Future.\nHow to make this code works?\nthanks.\n**The idea is to allow the next code in line triggered while http.get still doing its job.\nor should i move to Stream?\nfetchApi() need to return string instead of void.\n","excerpt":"print(&#39;start api&#39;);\n  for (var i = 1; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n    print(unawaited(fetchApi(i)));\n  }\n  print(&#39;end api&#39;);\n}\n\nFuture&lt;String&gt; fetchApi(int idNum) async {\n  final url = Uri.parse(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to properly use unawaited in dart?"},{"tags":["javascript","async-await","fetch-api"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":79942197,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79942195,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1778944350,"creation_date":1778944350,"body":"Your  is not the actual JSON yet because  also returns a Promise. You need to  it.\nUse this:\n\nAlso renember if you want to use the returned data outside the function, you must either  inside another async function or use :\n\n","excerpt":"Use this:\nasync function getUsers() {\n    try {\n        const response = await fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&quot;);\n\n        if (! &hellip; ","title":"Fetch API returning undefined data inside async function"},{"tags":["javascript","async-await","fetch-api"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79942195,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1778944350,"creation_date":1778944117,"body":"I am trying to fetch data from an API using JavaScript async/await, but the result is returning .\nHere is my code:\n\nInstead of getting the user data, I get a Promise object or undefined output.\nWhat am I doing wrong here, and how can I correctly retrieve the JSON data?\n","excerpt":"Here is my code:\nasync function getUsers() {\n    let response = await fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&quot;);\n    let data = response.json();\n\n    console.log(data);\n}\n\ngetUsers();\n\nInstead &hellip; ","title":"Fetch API returning undefined data inside async function"},{"tags":["javascript","async-await","promise","best-practices"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79937456,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1778150359,"creation_date":1778143280,"body":"Why is  faster than sequential  calls?\nQuestion\nI have two versions of a function.\nI understand that  runs both requests in parallel, but I would like to know:\n\nhow JavaScript schedules these requests internally\nwhether there are situations where sequential  is preferable\n\n\nHTML:\n\n","excerpt":"= await fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&#39;)\n    const users = await userResponse.json()\n\n    // Verkn&#252;pfung der Daten (Merging)\n    todos.forEach(t =&gt; {\n        // Sucht den User, dessen &hellip; const [todosRes, usersRes] = await Promise.all([\n        fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos&#39;),\n        fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&#39;)\n    ]);\n\n    // Umwandlung der &hellip; ","title":"How can fix this problem from json placeholder? ullrich"},{"tags":["javascript","async-await","promise","best-practices"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79937497,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79937456,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1778148157,"creation_date":1778148157,"body":"If you use\n\nJS waits for  to finish before starting .\nIts like \n\nIn the \n\nBoth requests are started immediately. JS does not download them itself. The network layer handles the HTTP requests asynchronously. The event loop continues running, and the await resumes only when both promises are resolved.\nIts like \n","excerpt":"Its like todos request time + users request time\n\nIn the Promise.all()\nconst [todosRes, usersRes] = await Promise.all([\n    fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos&#39;),\n    fetch(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How can fix this problem from json placeholder? ullrich"},{"tags":["javascript"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79937478,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79937456,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1778145597,"creation_date":1778145597,"body":"Also observe that  can (and should) be parallelized as well - you can do so with an helper function like:\n\nViel Spa&#223;!\n","excerpt":"function like:\nasync function fetchAPI(url) {\n  return fetch(url).then(response =&gt; response.json());\n}\n\nasync function getTodos() {\n  const [todos, users] = await Promise.all([\n    &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; /todos&#39;,\n    &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users&#39;\n  ].map(fetchAPI));\n\n  // more idiomatic than using forEach\n  return todos.map(t =&gt; ({\n    ...t,\n    user: users.find(u =&gt; u.id === t.userId) &hellip; ","title":"How can fix this problem from json placeholder? ullrich"},{"tags":["javascript"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79924681,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79924199,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1776071908,"creation_date":1776071908,"body":"Basically you want to be able to create a new Element via some  function:\n\nwithout necessarily having to store the above into i.e:  and that accepts additional options to go fetch children elements and append them once loaded.\nIf we start with this two utility functions:\n\nyou&#39;ll pretty quickly understand that what you actually need a  - capable of fetching and creating children from another resource - basically extending the  functionality:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","excerpt":"err.message);\n  }\n  return el;\n};\n\n// App\nconst app = {\n  init() {\n    elNewExt(&quot;div&quot;, {\n      className: &quot;header&quot;,\n      textContent: &quot;Loading...&quot;\n    }, /* Ext options: */ {\n      url: &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Asynchronous request and insertion of received nodes"},{"tags":["angular","typescript","signals"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79922794,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79922724,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1775735027,"creation_date":1775735027,"body":"No major difference between  and  with signals as far as I am aware.\nWhen you return  as a return value of  of any of the Angular Resource API.\nThe API call is not made, until a proper value is defined, which is one scenario, where  is preferential.\nBelow is an example, highlighting the similarities and resource API behaviour.\n\nStackblitz Demo\n","excerpt":"computation: () =&gt; `asdf123`,\n  });\n  undefinedResource = resource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.undefinedProp(),\n    stream: async () =&gt; {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; computation: () =&gt; `asdf123`,\n  });\n  nullResource = resource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.nullProp() || undefined,\n    stream: async ({}) =&gt; {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        &#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Should I use null or undefined as a default starting value for my signal?"},{"tags":["servicestack","servicestack-text"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79915685,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1775504669,"creation_date":1774612990,"body":"I have a simple ServiceStack client software:\n\nThis works as expected and outputs &quot;got 100&quot;\nWhen I add &lt;PublishAot&gt;true to my csproj file and publish it, I now get an error:\n\nDoes ServiceStack support aot? Is there something special I must do to get it to work?\n","excerpt":"I have a simple ServiceStack client software:\nvar client = new ServiceStack.JsonServiceClient\n{\n    BaseUri = &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/&quot;\n};\n\nvar results = await client.GetAsync&lt;List&lt;fake&gt;&gt; &hellip; ","title":"How do I use PublishAot with a ServiceStack client in the latest dotnet?"},{"tags":["angular","angular-signals","rxresource"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79899993,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79899937,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1772528824,"creation_date":1772528824,"body":"If you just want to make the API call without any signals being involved, you need to set the resource to a  value using the  method on the , then use  to trigger the API.\nIdeally there should be a flag or something, we can use to prevent the initial load. As on 03-03-2026 this is one possible way. Maybe it gets added in the future.\n\nStackblitz Demo\n","excerpt":"stream: () =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(`https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos/`);\n    },\n  });\n\n  constructor() {\n    this.resourceFetchedAlways.set([]);\n  }\n\n  ngOnInit() {\n    setTimeout &hellip; ","title":"Avoid initial request on rxResource creation?"},{"tags":["angular","angular-signals","rxresource"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":79899950,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79899937,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1772525078,"creation_date":1772524769,"body":"Yes we simply need to return  on the  (Greater than Angular 19) or  (Older than or equal to Angular 19). Until the required  are properly set to make the API call.\n\nFix: Return  to prevent the API to be triggered until ready.\nTS:\n\nHTML:\n\nStackblitz Demo\n\nIssue Demo: Without returning  always triggers the API on initialization (Error).\n\nStackblitz Demo\n","excerpt":"idData;\n      }\n      // return undefined to prevent API call trigger\n      return undefined;\n    },\n    stream: ({ params: { id } }) =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(\n        `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; = signal({ id: undefined });\n  resourceFetchedAlways = rxResource({\n    params: () =&gt; this.id(),\n    stream: ({ params: { id } }) =&gt; {\n      return this.httpClient.get(\n        `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Avoid initial request on rxResource creation?"},{"tags":["javascript","node.js","promise","axios"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":66510044,"is_answered":false,"question_id":66509721,"item_type":"answer","score":3,"last_activity_date":1771031509,"creation_date":1615060550,"body":"The fundamental problem is that  runs synchronously before the requests fire, so it&#39;s guaranteed to be empty.\n or  can help if you know how many pages you want up front (or if you&#39;re using a chunk size to make multiple requests--more on that later). These methods run in parallel. Here&#39;s a runnable proof-of-concept example:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe network tab shows the requests happening in parallel:\n\nIf the number of pages is unknown and you intend to fire requests one at a time until your API informs you of the end of the pages, a sequential loop is slow but can be used. Async/await is cleaner for this strategy:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere&#39;s the sequential request waterfall:\n\nA task queue or chunked loop can be used if you want to increase parallelization. p-limit or  are useful tools for limiting your  work. A chunked loop would combine the two techniques to request  records at a time and check each result in the chunk for the termination condition. Here&#39;s a simple example that strips out the filtering operation, which is sort of incidental to the asynchronous request issue and can be done synchronously after the responses arrive:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n(above image is an except of the 100 requests, but the chunk size of 5 at once is visible)\nNote that these snippets are proofs-of-concept and could stand to be less indiscriminate with catching errors, ensure all throws are caught, etc. When breaking it into sub-functions, make sure to  and  all promises in the caller--don&#39;t try to turn it into synchronous code.\nSee also\n\nHow do I return the response from an asynchronous call? and Why is my variable unaltered after I modify it inside of a function? - Asynchronous code reference which explain why the array is empty.\nWhat is the explicit promise construction antipattern and how do I avoid it?, which warns against adding a  to help resolve code that already returns promises.\n\n","excerpt":"then(response =&gt; // response has the token, ignored for simplicity\n    Promise.all(\n      Array(pages).fill().map((_, i) =&gt; // make an array of request promisess\n        axios.get(`https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; httpbin.org&quot;);\n  \n  const results = [];\n  \n  // page += 10 to make the snippet run faster; you&#39;d probably use page++\n  for (let page = 1;; page += 10) {\n    try {\n      const url = `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Array of filtered axios results from paginated API is empty"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs","react-hooks","use-effect","iife"],"question_score":10,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":6,"is_answered":true,"question_id":71480469,"item_type":"question","score":10,"last_activity_date":1771017343,"creation_date":1647339390,"body":"Is it good practice to use IIFE in useEffect or I should declare async function and then call it?\n\n","excerpt":"useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    (async () =&gt; {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&quot;\n      );\n      const json = await response.json();\n\n      setPosts(json); &hellip; ","title":"Should I use IIFE in useEffect hook?"},{"tags":["javascript","reactjs","react-hooks","use-effect","iife"],"question_score":10,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79889011,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71480469,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1771017343,"creation_date":1771015496,"body":"The use of an async IIFE is common and great to avoid &quot;callback hell&quot;.\nHowever, when setting state in it and if it depends on some state, you should always avoid race conditions by using a cleanup function.\nBy checking the flag at the end of the IIFE before synchronously setting all of your states, you ensure that you are not override a future call of the effect that happened to finish before you. It is important that all of your state changes ocur in one syncronous block, otherwise the flag could change between them (a time-of-check-time-of-use bug).\n\nA more performant approach where supported is to use a  to cancel the fetch early:\n\nLastly, you could use a library that is implemented to handle all of this for you automatically, efficiently, and correctly. Beyond the pitfalls discussed above with race conditions, there are opportunities for caching in more complex applications where the same fetch request may be used in multiple places, or if you want to keep the data cached for a period of time based on response headers, etc.\nThe most popular one as of 2026 is @tanstack/react-query, but other options exist as well:\n\n@tanstack/react-query - popular, mature, featureful\nswr - simple, small package, similar to tanstack query though not as featureful\n@apollo/client - large package, simpler ergonomics for graphql with &quot;normalized caching&quot; (meaning data responses from different requests can be cached for other requests based on the graph schema)\nrtk-query - the go-to solution for redux\nreact-router - full application router with built in data fetching/mutation (actions) ergonomics\n\n","excerpt":"change between them (a time-of-check-time-of-use bug).\nuseEffect(() =&gt; {\n  // flag to set at cleanup\n  let cancelled = false;\n  (async () =&gt; {\n    const response = await fetch(\n      &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; useEffect(() =&gt; {\n  // AbortController to abort at cleanup\n  const abortController = new AbortController();\n  (async () =&gt; {\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Should I use IIFE in useEffect hook?"},{"tags":["macos","flutter","http","flutter-http"],"question_score":141,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":65458903,"item_type":"question","score":141,"last_activity_date":1770172695,"creation_date":1609003570,"body":"I have following code in my Flutter app where  is a widget which starts up from \nCode:\n\nQuestion:\nAbove code is inspired from https://flutter.dev/docs/cookbook/networking/fetch-data. I get the following error when  widget loads up:\n\n[ERROR:flutter/lib/ui/ui_dart_state.cc(177)] Unhandled Exception:\nSocketException: Connection failed (OS Error: Operation not permitted,\nerrno = 1), address = jsonplaceholder.typicode.com, port = 443\n\nAbove error is of course because of the following line of code called when my widget is loading.\n\nfinal response = await\nhttp.get(&#39;https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/albums/1&#39;);\n\nBut, if I open https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/albums/1 on my browser, I am able to to see that it has dummy json which I expect to receive in the response. This means I am well connected to internet.\nI am running my app on macOS Big Sur version 11.1.\nWhat am I doing wrong? Do I need to declare some permissions for my app to be able to access internet from a macOS environment?\n","excerpt":"_MyDataLoaderState createState() =&gt; _MyDataLoaderState();\n}\n\nclass _MyDataLoaderState extends State&lt;MyDataLoader&gt; {\n  void getData() async {\n    final response = await http.get(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; final response = await\nhttp.get(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/albums/1&#39;);\n\nBut, if I open https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/albums/1 on my browser, I am able to to see that it has dummy json &hellip; ","title":"SocketException: Connection failed (OS Error: Operation not permitted, errno = 1) with flutter app on macOS"},{"tags":["python","concurrency","python-asyncio","aiohttp"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79801530,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1769532207,"creation_date":1761548256,"body":"I&#39;m trying to make a bunch of HTTP GET requests to different endpoints of the same API. My understanding is that  helps me manage concurrent I/O operations efficiently, meaning when one network request is waiting for data, the Python program can switch to another task instead of blocking. The goal is to maximize the overlap of these I/O wait times so that the total execution time is closer to the slowest individual request rather than the sum of all of them.\nHowever, it feels like my requests are not overlapping their I/O waits effectively. When I check the total time, it&#39;s roughly the sum of individual request times, not the time of the slowest request. I know  doesn&#39;t provide true parallelism like threads or separate processes do (it&#39;s still single-threaded on the Python side, so no bypassing the GIL for CPU-bound work), but for network-bound tasks, I expect significant concurrency.\nI&#39;m running Python 3.12.0.\nHere&#39;s the code I&#39;m using. I&#39;ve replaced my actual internal API with public placeholders, but the behavior persists with my own services.\n\nWhen I run this, I&#39;m getting output like this (timestamps will vary, but the pattern is the issue):\n\nThe issue is that the &quot;Starting request&quot; and &quot;Finished request&quot; print statements often appear sequentially, or with very little overlap, making the total runtime () close to the sum of individual request times (), rather than something much faster (like ~0.15-0.2s) as I&#39;d expect from concurrently managed network I/O.\nWhat am I missing here? Is there some common pitfall with &#39;s  or  that leads to this serialization for network-bound tasks, even with ? Could it be related to DNS resolution, TCP connection limits (either local or on the server), or something in my  setup?\nI&#39;ve checked:\n\nPython version (3.12.0)\n version ( shows )\nNo obvious  missing inside my  function.\n\n","excerpt":"/todos/1&quot;,\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos/2&quot;,\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/1&quot;,\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users/1&quot;,\n        &quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/1 in 0.1500s\n... and so on &hellip; ","title":"Why are my asyncio HTTP requests not overlapping as expected for I/O?"},{"tags":["python","concurrency","python-asyncio","aiohttp"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79877316,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79801530,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1769532207,"creation_date":1769532207,"body":"&quot;Works for me&quot;\nThe very snippet you put there performs concurrently as expected - here is my output\nwith Python 3.12:\n\nIf you are getting the same issues both with your own API and with the public server, these are being serialized in some other layer in your operating system. Maybe you have a firewall or antivirus, or VPN software which could be meddling with your requests and capping concurrent HTTP calls to the same domain?\nAh, I see you raised the question yourself:\n\nCould it be related to DNS resolution, TCP connection limits (either local or on the server), or something in my ClientSession setup?\n\nSo, given the results obtained, I&#39;d check your local TCP connection limits, from one of the possible sources above. DNS would not serialize the calls in this way, but otoh, it should be something easy for you to swap and try another one.\n(It also worked on Python 3.14, the output above is with 3.12.9).\n","excerpt":".$ python xis.py\n[1769531835.54] Starting request for: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos/2\n[1769531835.55] Starting &hellip; request for: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/users/1\n[1769531835.55] Starting request for: https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"Why are my asyncio HTTP requests not overlapping as expected for I/O?"},{"tags":["angular","advice"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79874962,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79874891,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1769218348,"creation_date":1769218348,"body":"It depends, on whether you want the original data to be unmodified and used elsewhere.\nKeep original data and use  for local changes:\nIf you have the scenario where you fetched the data using  and want to preserve the original data. As well as use the data for manipulations.\nYou can created a derived state using  (Which supports signal updates as well as replaces the state when the source signal changes).\nBelow is an example highlighting the usage of  to create a clone of the original data, which can be used for data manipulations.\nWhen we click the refresh button, the source signal updates and the linkedSignal (Local changes) are discarded.\nTS:\n\nHTML:\n\nStackblitz Demo\n\nAllow  data modifications (Source data need not be preserved):\nWhen you just want to manipulate the data, we can directly use the &#39;s  and  method to perform updates.\n\nSuppose you want to add an extra element during initialization. Then we can use the parse method to perform this initialization.\n\nThe most important thing to remember here is that. When the API is fetched and does not have any local changes, the resource status will always be . But when the resource is updated with local changes, the resource status will be .\nSo be mindful of this when writing  or  conditions using the resource API status signal ().\nFull Code:\n\nStackblitz Demo\n","excerpt":"TS:\n// original data. need to reuse in a dropdown.\ndata = httpResource&lt;any&gt;(() =&gt; `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos`, {\n  parse: (response: any) =&gt; {\n    return response.slice(0, 15);\n  },\n}) &hellip; Then we can use the parse method to perform this initialization.\ndata = httpResource&lt;any&gt;(() =&gt; `https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos`, {\n  parse: (response: any) =&gt; {\n    // add a default element &hellip; ","title":"Can you add extra item to data retrieved with httpResource?"},{"tags":["javascript","typescript","automated-tests","e2e-testing","playwright"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79869839,"is_answered":false,"question_id":67096646,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1768605896,"creation_date":1768605896,"body":"This would be easier to answer if the distinguishing info from the request or response bodies was provided. Nonetheless, I&#39;ll provide a minimal general example showing how to differentiate responses by both request and response bodies, since &#39;s predicate can be async, as of PR 6201.\n\nSee also: Playwright JavaScript: How to wait for a network request with a particular request payload?.\n","excerpt":"DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;script&gt;\nconst runSamplePost = id =&gt; {\n  return fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts&quot;, {\n    method: &quot;POST&quot;,\n    headers: {&quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;}, &hellip; ","title":"Playwright&#39;s page.waitForResponse differentiating by request and response bodies"},{"tags":["javascript","html","reactjs"],"question_score":6,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":71916440,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71915721,"item_type":"answer","score":13,"last_activity_date":1768385496,"creation_date":1650311620,"body":" is a very smart paradigm based on fetch-while-render strategy. This means that React Components from now on are able to consume directly asynchronous data. And will render the fallback while the data promise is not resolved.\nThe only issue is that you can&#39;t pass directly a promise, but you need a wrapper that transforms it into a . Basically the React Component wrapped in  tags, will start to try to render continuosly and it  expects for a method that throws a new promise until the original promise is not resolved, that&#39;s how it knows that it has to keep rendering the fallback. So you need to pass a resource with a very specific shape, that&#39;s why you need a wrapper.\nFetching libraries like react-queris or RTK-query will implement the wrapper themselves, so you won&#39;t have to care of that part, it&#39;s not something you will have to do manually, but still if you&#39;d like to see what&#39;s under the hood, this would be a very basic implementation of a  ready fetching library:\n\nThis is the custom hook  I made:\n\nSuspense\nThe working implementation is HERE\nIf you want to experiment how a Suspense-ready library works, you can check this example that makes use of the great state-manager  which is extremely light weight and easy to use, and . It let&#39;s you just pass a promise in your store, and when you try to access it in a component, the component will fallback if wrapped in .\nCheck it HERE\nEdit:\nAs someone in the comments noted, this example makes use of a custom hook using useEffect and useState to store the async suspense ready consumable entity, so it&#39;s not technically a real fetch-while-render since it executes only after the first render.\nYet this is one of the most stable and predictable approaches and it&#39;s used by some of the most used suspense-ready fetching libs like SWR, react-query or Apollo.\nNext.js server components make use instead of a straight fetch-while-render that is triggered immediately before the component paints, but it has to make use of a proper cache to behave properly.\nYou can try a mock HERE\n","excerpt":"&lt;/p&gt;}&gt;\n        &lt;User /&gt;\n      &lt;/Suspense&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport const User = () =&gt; {\n  const data = getData(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/todos/1&#39;);\n  return &lt;div&gt;{JSON.stringify(data)}&lt;/div &hellip; ","title":"How i can get suspense to work on react 18?"},{"tags":["javascript","html","reactjs"],"question_score":6,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":71915721,"item_type":"question","score":6,"last_activity_date":1768385496,"creation_date":1650306959,"body":"Hello my code is as follow wnna try suspense with react 18 but not having luck to render Loading....\nUser.jsx\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nApp.js\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nthe app renders {} or null but never getting the fallback as expected\n","excerpt":"User.jsx\n\r\n\r\nimport React, { useEffect, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nexport const User = () =&gt; {\n  const [user, setuser] = useState(null);\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    const res = fetch(&quot;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span> &hellip; ","title":"How i can get suspense to work on react 18?"},{"tags":["flutter","dart","dart-http"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79865111,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1768067520,"creation_date":1768065848,"body":"Dart/Flutter latest version,  module.\nAfter a successful http GET/POST, how to get the attributes of the ssl certificate of the server who made the response?\n\np.s. I&#39;m not searching how to do a &quot;certificate pinning&quot;, just read attributes.\n","excerpt":"Future&lt;Album&gt; fetchAlbum() async {\n  final response = await http.get(\n    Uri.parse(&#39;https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/albums/1&#39;),\n  );\n\n  if (response.statusCode == 200) {\n    // --&gt; Here I need to &hellip; ","title":"dart https request: read ssl server certificate fields"},{"tags":["angular","rxjs","tanstack"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79862799,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79524149,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1767824691,"creation_date":1767823746,"body":"Per your STR, I assume that query params changes in URL are observed via route observable that updates some signal connected to TanStack query flow.\nAs you didn&#39;t provide minimum code example I&#39;ve forked TanStack&#39;s public one, to demonstrate problem and solutions. It may differ in some ways, but the core concept should be the same.\nCanceling problem (stackblitz)\nEvery time  change, we send a new request without canceling the previous one.\n\nDirect abort signal solution (stackblitz)\nTo solve the problem we&#39;ll utilize the  abort signal. Per docs it states:\n\nWhen a query becomes out-of-date or inactive, this signal will become aborted.\n\nEvery time  changes, the abort signal will be triggered, and we can use it to cancel previous request.\n\nAbstracted abort signal solution (stackblitz)\nI&#39;m not a big fan of mixing API service flow with components. So, if you want to commit to TanStack and leave space for Angular&#39;s resources/rxResources I recommend abstracting  to service and exposing only signals similar to what you would do with  and observables. This will allow you to reuse entire flows via service in multiple components and scale/maintain logic easily.\n\n","excerpt":"#http\n            .get&lt;Post&gt;(`https://<span class=\"highlight\">jsonplaceholder.typicode.com</span>/posts/${postId()}`)\n            .pipe(takeUntil(aborters$))\n        );\n      },\n    }));\n  } &hellip; ","title":"How to cancel previous API calls when updating query params in Angular with TanStack Query"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":237}